  "decoder": "/root/blind_statement_decoder_39: independent source-blind mathematical statement decoder",
  "source_text_visible": false,
  "compiler_started": false,
  "reconstructed_theorem_text": "Let X and Y be arbitrary measurable spaces, and let mu and nu be probability measures on X and Y, respectively. Give X x Y its product measurable structure and the actual product measure mu x nu. Let F : X x Y -> R be measurable. Suppose F(x,y) >= 0 for every (x,y), and suppose there exists a real C such that F(x,y) <= C for every (x,y).\n\nDefine Phi(t) = t log(t) using the totalized real logarithm; in particular log(0) = 0 and Phi(0) = 0. Define the actual slice integrals A(x) = integral_Y F(x,y) dnu(y), B(y) = integral_X F(x,y) dmu(x), and the actual product integral m = integral_(X x Y) F(x,y) d(mu x nu)(x,y). Every integral here and below is a real Bochner integral.\n\nThen F and Phi composed with F are Bochner integrable under mu x nu. For every x in X, both y |-> F(x,y) and y |-> Phi(F(x,y)) are Bochner integrable under nu. For every y in Y, both x |-> F(x,y) and x |-> Phi(F(x,y)) are Bochner integrable under mu. A and Phi composed with A are Bochner integrable under mu, and B and Phi composed with B are Bochner integrable under nu. Moreover,\n\nintegral_X Phi(A(x)) dmu(x) + integral_Y Phi(B(y)) dnu(y)\n    <= integral_(X x Y) Phi(F(x,y)) d(mu x nu)(x,y) + Phi(m).\n\nThe integrability assertions and this inequality form one conjunctive conclusion. Every slice assertion holds for all points in the corresponding parameter space, not merely almost everywhere. There is no strictly positive lower bound on F or positive-mass assumption on m. The zero case uses the displayed totalized Phi directly, without a separate normalization or division by m.",
  "reconstructed_text_sha256": "ebb7de4551924b9fde975525d8b0f3e787a7f4f14b31acdfd682c7ba5b8bd897",
  "objects": "Arbitrary types X and Y equipped with measurable-space structures; actual probability measures mu on X and nu on Y; their product measure mu x nu; a real-valued function F on X x Y; Phi : R -> R defined by Phi(t)=t*log(t); A : X -> R and B : Y -> R given by the actual nu- and mu-slice integrals; m : R given by the actual full product integral. The upper bound C is an existential witness, not an additional datum in the inequality.",
  "domains": "F has domain the entire product X x Y with the product measurable structure. For fixed x in X its slice has domain all of Y and measure nu. For fixed y in Y its slice has domain all of X and measure mu. A and Phi(A) have domain X and measure mu; B and Phi(B) have domain Y and measure nu. F and Phi(F) are integrated against mu x nu. Phi is total on R; every argument actually used in the inequality is nonnegative. No topological, standard-Borel, smoothness or finite-dimensional structure is assumed.",
  "quantifiers": "Universally for every X, Y, their measurable structures, mu and nu that are probability measures, and every F satisfying the hypotheses, the full conjunctive conclusion holds. Measurability is assumed for F. Nonnegativity means for every z in X x Y, 0 <= F(z). Boundedness means there exists one real C such that for every z in X x Y, F(z) <= C. Slice integrability is universally quantified over every x in X and every y in Y, with no exceptional sets. A, B, m and Phi are let-defined objects, not extra independently quantified hypotheses.",
  "assumptions": "mu and nu are actual probability measures. F is measurable on the product, pointwise nonnegative everywhere, and pointwise bounded above everywhere by some real constant C. No initial integrability of F, slices, marginals or Phi transforms is assumed; each is supplied by the conclusion. No positive lower bound on F, strict positivity of A or B, or positive total mass m is assumed. No entropy, logarithmic-Sobolev, transport or regularity premise is present.",
  "conclusion": "The conjunction consists of: Integrable(F,mu x nu); Integrable(Phi(F),mu x nu); for every x, Integrable(y -> F(x,y),nu) and Integrable(y -> Phi(F(x,y)),nu); for every y, Integrable(x -> F(x,y),mu) and Integrable(x -> Phi(F(x,y)),mu); Integrable(A,mu); Integrable(B,nu); Integrable(Phi(A),mu); Integrable(Phi(B),nu); and the literal inequality integral_X Phi(A) dmu + integral_Y Phi(B) dnu <= integral_(X x Y) Phi(F) d(mu x nu) + Phi(m). Integrable means genuine Bochner integrability of the exact displayed functions under the exact displayed measures.",
  "scopes_senses": "All integrals are real Bochner integrals, including those defining A, B and m. Their initial totalized definitions do not replace the separate asserted genuine integrability. Phi uses the totalized real logarithm, with log(0)=0 and hence Phi(0)=0. Every pointwise assumption and every slice quantifier ranges over all points, not an almost-everywhere representative or quotient. The product and both marginal measures are exactly the probability measures displayed. No probability density normalization F/m, relative-entropy formula, almost-everywhere-only slice conclusion, logarithmic-Sobolev bound or transport conclusion is asserted. Zero-valued F and zero total mass are covered without division or a positivity proviso.",
  "constant_dependencies": "C may depend on F and its underlying spaces; the premise requires one uniform upper bound over the whole product. No prescribed value, independent universal upper bound, or strictly positive C is imposed. The concluding inequality has numerical coefficient 1 for each of its four terms, with no hidden constants depending on C, F, mu, nu, X or Y. Phi(m) is determined by the actual mass m, not an arbitrary correction constant.",
  "scopes": "All integrals are real Bochner integrals, including those defining A, B and m. Their initial totalized definitions do not replace the separate asserted genuine integrability. Phi uses the totalized real logarithm, with log(0)=0 and hence Phi(0)=0. Every pointwise assumption and every slice quantifier ranges over all points, not an almost-everywhere representative or quotient. The product and both marginal measures are exactly the probability measures displayed. No probability density normalization F/m, relative-entropy formula, almost-everywhere-only slice conclusion, logarithmic-Sobolev bound or transport conclusion is asserted. Zero-valued F and zero total mass are covered without division or a positivity proviso.",
